#: ../../apps/web/src/components/AziruIntroDialog.tsx:60
msgid "Amarnai is named after the <0>Amarna Letters</0>: clay cuneiform tablets used for diplomatic correspondence with the Egyptian pharaohs during the Bronze Age. One sender of these letters was Aziru, king of Amurru, a resourceful ruler and shrewd diplomat who navigated the rivalry between Egypt and the Hittites. Thousands of years later, he has found a new calling: helping folks like you bring order to their emails."
msgstr "Amarnai is vernoemd naar de <0>Amarna-brieven</0>: kleitabletten in spijkerschrift, gebruikt voor diplomatieke correspondentie met de Egyptische farao's tijdens de Bronstijd. Een van de afzenders van deze brieven was Aziru, koning van Amurru, een vindingrijke heerser en scherpzinnige diplomaat die slim navigeerde tussen Egypte en de Hettieten. Duizenden jaren later heeft hij een nieuwe roeping gevonden: mensen zoals jij helpen hun e-mails te ordenen."

#: ../../apps/site/src/components/landing/FAQSection.tsx:60
#~ msgid "Amarnai is named after the Amarna letters, a cache of clay tablets from around 1350 BC holding the diplomatic correspondence between the pharaoh of Egypt and the kings and vassals of the ancient Near East. They are one of the earliest surviving inboxes: a ruler working through hundreds of incoming messages, deciding what needed a reply and what could wait. Amarnai does the same for yours."
#~ msgstr ""

#: ../../apps/site/src/components/landing/FAQSection.tsx:60
#~ msgid "Amarnai is named after the Amarna letters, a cache of clay tablets from around 1350 BC holding the diplomatic correspondence between the pharaoh of Egypt and the kings and vassals of the ancient Near East. They are one of the earliest surviving inboxes: a ruler working through hundreds of incoming messages, deciding what needed a reply and what could wait. Tellingly, almost all of it is inbound. We have hundreds of letters to the pharaoh but barely any of his replies, and senders like Rib-Hadda of Byblos wrote again and again without an answer, most likely buried under an inbox no one could keep up with. Amarnai spares you that fate."
#~ msgstr ""

#: ../../apps/site/src/components/landing/FAQSection.tsx:60
#~ msgid "Amarnai is named after the Amarna letters, a cache of clay tablets from around 1350 BC: the diplomatic correspondence between the pharaoh of Egypt and the kings and vassals of the ancient Near East. It is one of the earliest surviving inboxes, and almost all of it is incoming. Hundreds of letters reached the pharaoh, but barely any of his replies survive. Rib-Hadda of Byblos wrote again and again and went unanswered, most likely lost in an inbox no one could keep up with. Amarnai keeps yours from that fate."
#~ msgstr ""

#: ../../apps/site/src/components/landing/FAQSection.tsx:60
#, fuzzy
msgid "Amarnai is named after the Amarna letters, a cache of clay tablets from around 1350 BC: the diplomatic correspondence between the pharaoh of Egypt and the kings and vassals of the ancient Near East. It is one of the earliest surviving inboxes, and almost all of it is incoming. Hundreds of letters reached the pharaoh, but barely any of his replies survive. Senders like Rib-Hadda of Byblos wrote again and again with little response, most likely because the pharaoh was too overloaded to keep up. Amarnai spares you from that fate."
msgstr "Amarnai is vernoemd naar de Amarnabrieven, een verzameling kleitabletten uit circa 1350 v.Chr.: de diplomatieke correspondentie tussen de farao van Egypte en de koningen en vazallen uit het Oude Nabije Oosten. Het is een van de vroegste bewaard gebleven 'inboxen', en bijna alle berichten zijn inkomend. Honderden brieven bereikten de farao, maar van zijn antwoorden is nauwelijks iets bewaard gebleven. Afzenders zoals Rib-Hadda van Byblos schreven keer op keer zonder veel reactie, waarschijnlijk omdat de farao te overladen was om alles bij te houden. Amarnai behoedt u voor dat lot."

